Function Returns one row as Read it with <code>mysqli_fetch_assoc($r)</code> an associative array <code>$row["name"]</code> — the one to use <code>mysqli_fetch_row($r)</code> a numbered array <code>$row[0]</code> <code>mysqli_fetch_array($r)</code> both at once, by default wasteful; pass <code>MYSQLI_ASSOC</code> if you use it <code>mysqli_fetch_object($r)</code> an object <code>$row-&gt;name</code> <code>mysqli_fetch_all($r, MYSQLI_ASSOC)</code> every row at once an array of arrays — handy for <code>json_encode</code> in Unit 5 <p>Each call returns the next row and moves on; when there are no more it returns <code>null</code>, which is why <code>while ($row = mysqli_fetch_assoc($result))</code> is the standard loop — the assignment's value is the row, and null ends the loop.</p> <p>Listing 4.14. All five fetch styles on one query</p> <pre><code>&lt;?php
$conn = mysqli_connect("localhost", "root", "", "college");
$sql  = "SELECT roll, name, marks FROM students ORDER BY roll LIMIT 2";

echo "fetch_assoc:\n";
$r = mysqli_query($conn, $sql);
while ($row = mysqli_fetch_assoc($r)) echo "  {$row['name']} ({$row['roll']})\n";

echo "\nfetch_row:\n";
$r = mysqli_query($conn, $sql);
while ($row = mysqli_fetch_row($r)) echo "  $row[1] ($row[0])\n";

echo "\nfetch_object:\n";
$r = mysqli_query($conn, $sql);
while ($row = mysqli_fetch_object($r)) echo "  {$row-&gt;name} ({$row-&gt;roll})\n";

echo "\nfetch_all:\n";
$r = mysqli_query($conn, $sql);
print_r(mysqli_fetch_all($r, MYSQLI_ASSOC));
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>fetch_assoc:
  Zoya Khan (2231045)
  Aman Verma (2231046)

fetch_row:
  Zoya Khan (2231045)
  Aman Verma (2231046)

fetch_object:
  Zoya Khan (2231045)
  Aman Verma (2231046)

fetch_all:
Array
(
    [0] =&gt; Array
        (
            [roll] =&gt; 2231045
            [name] =&gt; Zoya Khan
            [marks] =&gt; 82
        )

    [1] =&gt; Array
        (
            [roll] =&gt; 2231046
            [name] =&gt; Aman Verma
            [marks] =&gt; 67
        )

)
</code></pre> <p>Same rows, five shapes. <code>fetch_assoc</code> is right almost always: the code says <code>$row["marks"]</code>, which still means something when somebody adds a column.</p>

Attribute On Does <code>name</code> every input The key in <code>$_POST</code>. No name, no data — this is the single most common reason a field "does not arrive" <code>action</code> the form which page receives it. Empty or omitted means this same page <code>method</code> the form <code>get</code> (the default) or <code>post</code> <code>value</code> every input the starting value — and for radios and checkboxes, the value that gets sent <code>id</code> + <code>&lt;label for&gt;</code> accessibility clicking the label focuses the field. Nothing to do with PHP <code>required</code>, <code>pattern</code>, <code>min</code> inputs browser-side checks. Helpful, and not security <code>enctype="multipart/form-data"</code> the form needed only when uploading a file <p>Listing 3.1. A working form</p> <p><code>index.php</code></p> <pre><code>&lt;h3&gt;Student registration&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;form action="save.php" method="post"&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;
    &lt;label for="r"&gt;Roll number&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input type="text" id="r" name="rollno" value="2231045"&gt;
  &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;
    &lt;label for="n"&gt;Name&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input type="text" id="n" name="sname" value="Zoya Khan"&gt;
  &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;
    &lt;label for="m"&gt;Marks&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input type="number" id="m" name="marks" value="82"&gt;
  &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;button type="submit"&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
</code></pre> <p><code>save.php</code></p> <pre><code>&lt;?php
// Everything the form sent arrives here, in $_POST
$roll  = $_POST["rollno"] ?? "";
$name  = $_POST["sname"]  ?? "";
$marks = $_POST["marks"]  ?? "";
?&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;Received&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;?= htmlspecialchars($name) ?&gt;&lt;/b&gt; (roll &lt;?= htmlspecialchars($roll) ?&gt;)
   scored &lt;?= htmlspecialchars($marks) ?&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;The whole parcel&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;?php print_r($_POST); ?&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="index.php"&gt;&amp;larr; back to the form&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</code></pre> <p>Output sent to the browser:</p> <pre><code>&lt;h3&gt;Student registration&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;form action="save.php" method="post"&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;
    &lt;label for="r"&gt;Roll number&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input type="text" id="r" name="rollno" value="2231045"&gt;
  &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;
    &lt;label for="n"&gt;Name&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input type="text" id="n" name="sname" value="Zoya Khan"&gt;
  &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;
    &lt;label for="m"&gt;Marks&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input type="number" id="m" name="marks" value="82"&gt;
  &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;button type="submit"&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
</code></pre> <p>Submitting the form as it stands sends <code>rollno</code>, <code>sname</code> and <code>marks</code> to <code>save.php</code>, which replies:</p> <p>The reply:</p> <pre><code>&lt;h3&gt;Received&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Zoya Khan&lt;/b&gt; (roll 2231045)
   scored 82.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;The whole parcel&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;Array
(
    [rollno] =&gt; 2231045
    [sname] =&gt; Zoya Khan
    [marks] =&gt; 82
)
&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="index.php"&gt;&amp;larr; back to the form&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</code></pre> <p>The form posts three named values and <code>save.php</code> reads them from <code>$_POST</code>. The field names in <code>index.php</code> and the keys used in <code>save.php</code> must agree, because the names are the contract between the two files.</p> <p>Three things to notice</p> <p>The <code>name</code> attributes became the array keys. Every value is a string — <code>"82"</code>, not <code>82</code>, even though the input was <code>type="number"</code>. And <code>?? ""</code> is there so that reaching <code>save.php</code> directly, with no form behind it, gives an empty string rather than a warning.</p>

Key Is Trust it? <code>name</code> the file name on the visitor's machine No. It is a string the browser sent; it can contain anything <code>type</code> the MIME type the browser claimed No. Anyone can claim <code>image/jpeg</code> <code>tmp_name</code> where PHP parked it on the server Yes — this is PHP's own value, and the file is deleted when the script ends <code>size</code> bytes actually received Yes <code>error</code> <code>0</code> = <code>UPLOAD_ERR_OK</code>; other codes explain the failure Yes — check this first, always <p>Listing 3.11. An upload handler, with every check</p> <pre><code>&lt;?php
// The form that produced this had enctype="multipart/form-data".

if (!isset($_FILES["photo"])) {
    exit("No file field was submitted at all.\n");
}

$file    = $_FILES["photo"];
$maxSize = 2 * 1024 * 1024;                    // 2 MB
$allowed = ["jpg", "jpeg", "png", "gif"];

echo "What arrived:\n";
print_r($file);
echo "\n";

// 1. the error code comes first — everything else is meaningless without it
if ($file["error"] !== UPLOAD_ERR_OK) {
    $why = [
        UPLOAD_ERR_INI_SIZE  =&gt; "larger than upload_max_filesize in php.ini",
        UPLOAD_ERR_FORM_SIZE =&gt; "larger than the form's MAX_FILE_SIZE",
        UPLOAD_ERR_PARTIAL   =&gt; "only partly uploaded",
        UPLOAD_ERR_NO_FILE   =&gt; "no file was chosen",
    ];
    exit("Upload failed: " . ($why[$file["error"]] ?? "error " . $file["error"]) . "\n");
}

// 2. size — the browser's own limit is advisory, so check it here
if ($file["size"] &gt; $maxSize) {
    exit("Too large: " . round($file["size"] / 1024) . " KB, limit 2048 KB\n");
}

// 3. the extension, taken from the name and lower-cased
$ext = strtolower(pathinfo($file["name"], PATHINFO_EXTENSION));
if (!in_array($ext, $allowed, true)) {
    exit("Type '$ext' is not allowed. Permitted: " . implode(", ", $allowed) . "\n");
}

// 4. was it really uploaded by PHP, or is someone naming a system file?
if (!is_uploaded_file($file["tmp_name"])) {
    exit("That is not an uploaded file.\n");
}

// 5. invent our own name. NEVER reuse the one the browser sent.
$newName = "photo_" . date("Ymd") . "_" . uniqid() . "." . $ext;
$target  = "uploads/" . $newName;

if (move_uploaded_file($file["tmp_name"], $target)) {
    echo "Accepted.\n";
    echo "  stored as : $target\n";
    echo "  size      : " . round($file["size"] / 1024) . " KB\n";
    echo "  original  : " . htmlspecialchars($file["name"]) . "  (kept only for display)\n";
} else {
    echo "Could not move the file — check that uploads/ exists and is writable.\n";
}
</code></pre> <p>The script is run with this uploaded file, as the browser would report it:</p> <pre><code>{
  "photo": {
    "name": "zoya holiday.JPG",
    "type": "image/jpeg",
    "tmp_name": "/tmp/php7A2c",
    "error": 0,
    "size": 184320
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>What arrived:
Array
(
    [name] =&gt; zoya holiday.JPG
    [type] =&gt; image/jpeg
    [tmp_name] =&gt; /tmp/php7A2c
    [error] =&gt; 0
    [size] =&gt; 184320
)

Accepted.
  stored as : uploads/photo_20261109_6540b1f3a2c9d.jpg
  size      : 180 KB
  original  : zoya holiday.JPG  (kept only for display)
</code></pre> <p>The script is given a real <code>$_FILES</code> array, so the validation runs for real. Changing the values in the array (setting <code>error</code> to 1, making the size huge, or renaming the file to <code>shell.php</code>) makes a different check catch the upload.</p> <p>Three rules that are not optional</p> <p>1. Never save the file under the name the browser sent. A name like <code>../../index.php</code> or <code>shell.php</code> is a request to overwrite your site or to run the visitor's code. Generate your own name, as above. 2. Never put uploads in a folder the web server will execute. Either store them outside the document root and serve them through a PHP script, or make sure the folder cannot run PHP. 3. Never believe <code>$_FILES["photo"]["type"]</code>. It is whatever the browser said. Check the extension against a whitelist, and on a real server verify the content too with <code>finfo_file()</code> or <code>getimagesize()</code>.</p> <p>the form that goes with it</p> <pre><code>&lt;form action="upload.php" method="post" enctype="multipart/form-data"&gt;

  &lt;!-- advisory only: the browser may ignore it, so php.ini and the
       handler are the real limits --&gt;
  &lt;input type="hidden" name="MAX_FILE_SIZE" value="2097152"&gt;

  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label for="p"&gt;Photograph&lt;/label&gt;
     &lt;input type="file" id="p" name="photo" accept="image/*"&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

  &lt;p&gt;&lt;button type="submit"&gt;Upload&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
</code></pre> <p>Two settings in <code>php.ini</code> cap everything above: <code>upload_max_filesize</code> (2 MB by default) and <code>post_max_size</code> (8 MB). If a large file silently produces an empty <code>$_POST</code> and an empty <code>$_FILES</code>, it exceeded <code>post_max_size</code> — a confusing failure worth recognising.</p> <p>Several files at once</p> <p><code>&lt;input type="file" name="docs[]" multiple&gt;</code> gives a differently shaped array: <code>$_FILES["docs"]["name"]</code> is itself an array, and so is <code>["tmp_name"]</code>, <code>["error"]</code> and <code>["size"]</code>. So you loop with <code>foreach ($_FILES["docs"]["name"] as $i =&gt; $name)</code> and index every other key with the same <code>$i</code>. It catches people out because the nesting is the opposite way round from what you would expect.</p>

Function Does Example <code>count($a)</code> How many elements <code>count([1,2,3])</code> → 3 <code>array_push($a, $v)</code> / <code>$a[] = $v</code> Append the second form is preferred <code>array_pop($a)</code> Remove and return the last a stack <code>array_shift($a)</code> / <code>array_unshift($a,$v)</code> Remove from / add to the front a queue <code>in_array($v, $a)</code> Is this value present? returns <code>true</code>/<code>false</code> <code>array_search($v, $a)</code> Which key holds it? returns the key, or <code>false</code> <code>array_keys($a)</code> / <code>array_values($a)</code> Just the keys / just the values renumbers from 0 <code>array_merge($a, $b)</code> Join two arrays string keys overwrite, integer keys renumber <code>array_slice($a, 2, 3)</code> A piece of it used for pagination in Unit 4 <code>implode(", ", $a)</code> Array → string the opposite of <code>explode</code> <code>array_sum</code> / <code>array_product</code> Add / multiply everything totals <code>array_column($rows, "name")</code> One column out of a table invaluable with database rows <code>array_unique($a)</code> Drop duplicates keeps the first, preserves keys <code>sort</code>, <code>rsort</code> Sort by value, renumber the keys for indexed arrays <code>asort</code>, <code>arsort</code> Sort by value, keep the keys for associative arrays <code>ksort</code>, <code>krsort</code> Sort by key <code>usort($a, $fn)</code> Sort by your own rule the callback returns -1, 0 or 1 <p>Listing 1.12. Sorting: which function keeps the keys?</p> <pre><code>&lt;?php
$marks = ["Zoya" =&gt; 82, "Aman" =&gt; 67, "Riya" =&gt; 91, "Dev" =&gt; 55];

$a = $marks; sort($a);
echo "sort()   — value order, keys thrown away:\n"; print_r($a);

$b = $marks; asort($b);
echo "\nasort()  — value order, keys kept:\n"; print_r($b);

$c = $marks; arsort($c);
echo "\narsort() — highest first, keys kept (the class rank list):\n"; print_r($c);

$d = $marks; ksort($d);
echo "\nksort()  — alphabetical by name:\n"; print_r($d);

// Your own rule: longest name first, then alphabetical
$names = array_keys($marks);
usort($names, function ($x, $y) {
    return (strlen($y) &lt;=&gt; strlen($x)) ?: ($x &lt;=&gt; $y);
});
echo "\nusort() with a custom rule:\n"; print_r($names);
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>sort()   — value order, keys thrown away:
Array
(
    [0] =&gt; 55
    [1] =&gt; 67
    [2] =&gt; 82
    [3] =&gt; 91
)

asort()  — value order, keys kept:
Array
(
    [Dev] =&gt; 55
    [Aman] =&gt; 67
    [Zoya] =&gt; 82
    [Riya] =&gt; 91
)

arsort() — highest first, keys kept (the class rank list):
Array
(
    [Riya] =&gt; 91
    [Zoya] =&gt; 82
    [Aman] =&gt; 67
    [Dev] =&gt; 55
)

ksort()  — alphabetical by name:
Array
(
    [Aman] =&gt; 67
    [Dev] =&gt; 55
    [Riya] =&gt; 91
    [Zoya] =&gt; 82
)

usort() with a custom rule:
Array
(
    [0] =&gt; Aman
    [1] =&gt; Riya
    [2] =&gt; Zoya
    [3] =&gt; Dev
)
</code></pre> <p>The rule to memorise: the a in <code>asort</code> is for associative — it keeps keys attached. Plain <code>sort()</code> throws the keys away and renumbers from zero, which destroys an associative array.</p> <p>Listing 1.13. Arrays are copied, not shared</p> <pre><code>&lt;?php
$a = [1, 2, 3];
$b = $a;        // a copy
$b[] = 4;

echo "a has ", count($a), " elements, b has ", count($b), "\n";

$c = &amp;$a;       // a reference — the same array under two names
$c[] = 99;
echo "after \$c[] = 99, a has ", count($a), " elements\n";

// Passing to a function also copies, unless the parameter is declared &amp;
function addItem($arr)      { $arr[] = "x"; return count($arr); }
function addItemRef(&amp;$arr)  { $arr[] = "x"; return count($arr); }

$list = ["p", "q"];
addItem($list);     echo "after addItem:    ", count($list), "\n";
addItemRef($list);  echo "after addItemRef: ", count($list), "\n";
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>a has 3 elements, b has 4
after $c[] = 99, a has 4 elements
after addItem:    2
after addItemRef: 3
</code></pre> <p>This is the difference between PHP and JavaScript that trips people up. If you genuinely want to share, write <code>$b = &amp;$a</code> — but you rarely do.</p>

Operator True when Result for <code>"5"</code> and <code>5</code> <code>==</code> equal after type juggling true <code>===</code> equal and the same type false <code>!=</code> or <code>&lt;&gt;</code> not equal, loosely false <code>!==</code> not equal, or a different type true <code>&lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;=</code> ordering, loosely — <code>&lt;=&gt;</code> spaceship: −1, 0 or 1 0 <p>Use <code>===</code> whenever <code>false</code> could be a real answer</p> <p><code>strpos("hello", "h")</code> returns <code>0</code> — the position — and <code>0 == false</code> is true. So <code>if (strpos($s, $x) == false)</code> reports "not found" for a match at the very start. Writing <code>=== false</code> fixes it. The same trap waits in <code>array_search()</code>.</p> <p>Listing 1.14. The strpos trap, and the fix</p> <pre><code>&lt;?php
$email = "hod@ex.ac.in";

// Wrong: 'h' is at position 0, and 0 == false
if (strpos($email, "hod") == false) {
    echo "WRONG:  it says 'hod' is not there\n";
}

// Right:
if (strpos($email, "hod") === false) {
    echo "not found\n";
} else {
    echo "RIGHT:  found at position ", strpos($email, "hod"), "\n";
}

// Since PHP 8 there is a function with no trap at all:
var_dump(str_contains($email, "hod"));
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>WRONG:  it says 'hod' is not there
RIGHT:  found at position 0
bool(true)
</code></pre> <p>This is the single most examined gotcha in PHP. The rule: any function that can return <code>0</code> or <code>false</code> must be tested with <code>===</code>.</p>

Operator True when Note <code>&amp;&amp;</code> / <code>and</code> both Short-circuits: if the left is false the right is never evaluated <code>\|\|</code> / <code>or</code> either Short-circuits the other way <code>!</code> the opposite <code>xor</code> exactly one rare <p><code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>and</code> mean the same thing but bind differently: <code>and</code> binds looser than <code>=</code>, so <code>$ok = true and false;</code> assigns <code>true</code>. Use <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code> and the problem never arises.</p> <p>Listing 1.15. Short-circuiting is a feature, not a detail</p> <pre><code>&lt;?php
function noisy($label, $value) {
    echo "  (evaluated $label)\n";
    return $value;
}

echo "false &amp;&amp; ... :\n";
$r = noisy("left", false) &amp;&amp; noisy("right", true);   // right is skipped

echo "\ntrue || ... :\n";
$r = noisy("left", true) || noisy("right", true);    // right is skipped

echo "\nThe practical use — guarding an array key:\n";
$data = ["marks" =&gt; 45];
if (isset($data["bonus"]) &amp;&amp; $data["bonus"] &gt; 10) {
    echo "  bonus applies\n";
} else {
    echo "  no bonus, and no warning either\n";
}

echo "\nAnd the precedence trap:\n";
$a = true and false;   // = binds tighter than 'and'
$b = true &amp;&amp; false;
var_dump($a, $b);
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>false &amp;&amp; ... :
  (evaluated left)

true || ... :
  (evaluated left)

The practical use — guarding an array key:
  no bonus, and no warning either

And the precedence trap:
bool(false)
bool(false)
</code></pre> <p>Short-circuiting is what makes <code>isset($a['k']) &amp;&amp; $a['k'] &gt; 5</code> safe: if the key is missing, the second half never runs, so there is no warning.</p>

Method Does Note <code>.text()</code> get or set the text escapes markup — use this for anything a user typed <code>.html()</code> get or set the inner HTML renders tags; an XSS hole if the content came from a user <code>.val()</code> get or set a form field's value the one for <code>input</code>, <code>select</code>, <code>textarea</code> <code>.attr("href")</code> get or set an attribute <code>.prop("checked")</code> get or set a live property use for <code>checked</code>, <code>disabled</code>, <code>selected</code> <code>.css("color", "red")</code> set a style fine for one property; a class is better for many <code>.addClass</code> / <code>.removeClass</code> / <code>.toggleClass</code> change classes the tidy way to restyle <code>.append</code> / <code>.prepend</code> add inside, at the end / start <code>.after</code> / <code>.before</code> add outside <code>.remove()</code> / <code>.empty()</code> delete the element / its contents <code>.each(fn)</code> loop over the matches <code>$(this)</code> is the current one <code>.show()</code> / <code>.hide()</code> / <code>.toggle()</code> visibility add a duration for a fade <code>.fadeIn</code> / <code>.slideUp</code> / <code>.animate</code> effects chainable and queued <p>Chaining</p> <p>Every method that changes something returns the same jQuery object, so calls can be strung together: <code>$("#msg").text("Saved").css("color", "green").fadeIn(300);</code>. That is why jQuery code reads as one sentence per line.</p> <p>Listing 5.3. Every DOM method, on one page</p> <p>HTML</p> <pre><code>&lt;div id="box" style="border:2px solid #4a4a9c;border-radius:8px;padding:10px;min-height:60px"&gt;
  &lt;p id="para"&gt;The original paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
  &lt;button data-do="text"&gt;.text()&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button data-do="html"&gt;.html()&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button data-do="append"&gt;.append()&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button data-do="class"&gt;.toggleClass()&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button data-do="attr"&gt;.attr()&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button data-do="fade"&gt;.fadeToggle()&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button data-do="empty"&gt;.empty()&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button data-do="reset"&gt;reset&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;Type here: &lt;input id="src" value="&amp;lt;b&amp;gt;bold&amp;lt;/b&amp;gt;"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p id="log" style="font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;color:#12808f"&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</code></pre> <p>CSS</p> <pre><code>.fancy { background:#fcf1de; border-left:5px solid #e2952f; padding-left:10px; }
</code></pre> <p>JavaScript</p> <pre><code>$(function () {

    var original = $("#box").html();

    $("button").on("click", function () {
        var what  = $(this).data("do");
        var input = $("#src").val();

        switch (what) {
            case "text":
                $("#para").text(input);
                say(".text() showed it as characters — the tags are visible");
                break;

            case "html":
                $("#para").html(input);
                say(".html() parsed it as markup — the tags took effect");
                break;

            case "append":
                $("#box").append("&lt;p&gt;Appended at " + new Date().toLocaleTimeString() + "&lt;/p&gt;");
                say("#box now holds " + $("#box p").length + " paragraphs");
                break;

            case "class":
                $("#para").toggleClass("fancy");
                say("class list: " + ($("#para").attr("class") || "(none)"));
                break;

            case "attr":
                $("#para").attr("title", "set by jQuery").css("cursor", "help");
                say("hover the paragraph to see the title attribute");
                break;

            case "fade":
                $("#para").fadeToggle(300);
                say("fadeToggle animates opacity, then sets display:none");
                break;

            case "empty":
                $("#box").empty();
                say(".empty() removed the children but kept #box itself");
                break;

            case "reset":
                $("#box").html(original);
                say("back to the start");
                break;
        }
    });

    function say(msg) { $("#log").text(msg); }
});
</code></pre> <p>Each button applies one DOM method to the box. <code>.text()</code> shows the typed string as characters, so the tags are visible, whereas <code>.html()</code> parses it as markup and the word becomes bold: the same string, two very different results. <code>.append()</code> adds a paragraph, <code>.toggleClass()</code> adds or removes the class <code>fancy</code>, <code>.attr()</code> sets a <code>title</code> attribute, <code>.fadeToggle()</code> animates the paragraph away and back, <code>.empty()</code> removes the children of the box, and the last button restores the original.</p> <p><code>.html()</code> with user input is the same hole as <code>echo $_POST</code></p> <p><code>$("#out").html(userText)</code> renders whatever tags are in it, in the visitor's browser. Unit 3 taught <code>htmlspecialchars()</code> for the server side; on the client side the equivalent is simply to use <code>.text()</code>. Reach for <code>.html()</code> only when you built the string.</p>

Written as Fires when <code>$(sel).on("click", fn)</code> the modern, general form — use this <code>$(sel).click(fn)</code> a shorthand for the same thing <code>"submit"</code> a form is submitted — on the <code>&lt;form&gt;</code>, not the button <code>"change"</code> a select, checkbox or radio changes <code>"input"</code>, <code>"keyup"</code> the user types <code>"focus"</code>, <code>"blur"</code> a field is entered / left <code>"mouseenter"</code>, <code>"mouseleave"</code> hover <code>"dblclick"</code> double click <p>Inside a handler, <code>this</code> is the plain DOM element and <code>$(this)</code> wraps it so jQuery methods work. <code>e.preventDefault()</code> stops the default behaviour — the form submitting, the link navigating.</p> <p>Listing 5.4. Events, including delegation</p> <p>HTML</p> <pre><code>&lt;p&gt;
  &lt;button id="add"&gt;Add a row&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;label style="margin-left:12px"&gt;
    &lt;input type="checkbox" id="use-delegation" checked&gt; use delegation
  &lt;/label&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul id="list"&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Original row &lt;button class="del"&gt;delete&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;Live search: &lt;input id="q" placeholder="type to filter"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p id="log" style="font-family:monospace;font-size:13px;color:#12808f"&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</code></pre> <p>JavaScript</p> <pre><code>$(function () {

    var n = 1;

    // ---- delegated: the handler lives on #list, which always exists -----
    $("#list").on("click", ".del", function () {
        if (!$("#use-delegation").prop("checked")) return;
        $(this).closest("li").remove();
        log("deleted by the DELEGATED handler");
    });

    // ---- direct: bound only to the buttons that existed just now -------
    $(".del").on("click", function () {
        if ($("#use-delegation").prop("checked")) return;
        $(this).closest("li").remove();
        log("deleted by the DIRECT handler");
    });

    $("#add").on("click", function () {
        n++;
        $("#list").append("&lt;li&gt;Row " + n + " &lt;button class='del'&gt;delete&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/li&gt;");
        log("added row " + n + " — try deleting it with each setting");
    });

    // ---- typing ---------------------------------------------------------
    $("#q").on("input", function () {
        var term = $(this).val().toLowerCase();
        $("#list li").each(function () {
            var match = $(this).text().toLowerCase().indexOf(term) &gt; -1;
            $(this).toggle(match);
        });
        log("filtering on: " + (term || "(nothing)"));
    });

    function log(m) { $("#log").text(m); }
});
</code></pre> <p>Adding a row and then deleting the new row shows the difference between the two kinds of handler. With delegation, the handler lives on the list, which always exists, so it works on rows added later. Without it, the handler was bound only to the buttons that existed when the page loaded, so the new row's button does nothing. This is the single most common jQuery bug. The search box filters the list as the user types.</p> <p>Why delegation is needed</p> <p><code>$(".del").on("click", …)</code> finds the buttons that exist at that moment and attaches a handler to each. A button created later never had one attached. <code>$("#list").on("click", ".del", …)</code> attaches one handler to the list, which checks on each click whether what was clicked matches <code>.del</code> — so it works for rows added at any time. Any list you build from AJAX data needs this.</p> <p>Listing 5.5. A form validated without leaving the page</p> <p>HTML</p> <pre><code>&lt;form id="reg"&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;Name &lt;input name="sname" id="sname"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
     &lt;span class="err" id="e-sname"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;Email &lt;input name="email" id="email"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
     &lt;span class="err" id="e-email"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;Marks &lt;input name="marks" id="marks"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;
     &lt;span class="err" id="e-marks"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;button type="submit"&gt;Register&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
&lt;div id="ok"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre> <p>CSS</p> <pre><code>.err   { color:#c0392b; font-size:13px; margin-left:8px; }
.bad   { border-color:#c0392b !important; background:#fdf0ee; }
#ok    { color:#1a7f4b; font-weight:600; }
</code></pre> <p>JavaScript</p> <pre><code>$(function () {

    $("#reg").on("submit", function (e) {
        e.preventDefault();            // stop the page reloading
        $(".err").text("");
        $("input").removeClass("bad");
        $("#ok").text("");

        var ok = true;

        var name = $.trim($("#sname").val());
        if (name.length &lt; 3) {
            fail("#sname", "at least 3 characters");
            ok = false;
        }

        var email = $.trim($("#email").val());
        if (!/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[a-z]{2,}$/i.test(email)) {
            fail("#email", "not a valid address");
            ok = false;
        }

        var marks = $("#marks").val();
        if (marks === "" || isNaN(marks) || marks &lt; 0 || marks &gt; 100) {
            fail("#marks", "a number from 0 to 100");
            ok = false;
        }

        if (ok) {
            $("#ok").text("All good — this is where the AJAX call would go.");
        }
    });

    function fail(sel, msg) {
        $(sel).addClass("bad");
        $("#e-" + sel.substring(1)).text(msg);
    }
});
</code></pre> <p>Submitting the form empty, or with a bad email address, shows a message beside each faulty field without the page reloading, because <code>e.preventDefault()</code> cancels the browser's own submission. A valid submission shows "All good — this is where the AJAX call would go." Remember Unit 3: this is a convenience, and the server must still check.</p>

Procedural Object Does <code>mysqli_connect($h,$u,$p,$db)</code> <code>new mysqli($h,$u,$p,$db)</code> connect <code>mysqli_connect_error()</code> <code>$conn-&gt;connect_error</code> why it failed <code>mysqli_query($conn,$sql)</code> <code>$conn-&gt;query($sql)</code> run; <code>false</code> on failure <code>mysqli_error($conn)</code> <code>$conn-&gt;error</code> the message <code>mysqli_fetch_assoc($r)</code> <code>$r-&gt;fetch_assoc()</code> next row as an array <code>mysqli_num_rows($r)</code> <code>$r-&gt;num_rows</code> rows in a SELECT <code>mysqli_affected_rows($conn)</code> <code>$conn-&gt;affected_rows</code> rows changed — takes the connection <code>mysqli_insert_id($conn)</code> <code>$conn-&gt;insert_id</code> the AUTO_INCREMENT just used <code>mysqli_prepare($conn,$sql)</code> <code>$conn-&gt;prepare($sql)</code> a prepared statement <code>mysqli_stmt_bind_param($s,"is",…)</code> <code>$stmt-&gt;bind_param("is",…)</code> i s d b, one per <code>?</code> <code>mysqli_stmt_execute($s)</code> <code>$stmt-&gt;execute()</code> run it <code>mysqli_stmt_get_result($s)</code> <code>$stmt-&gt;get_result()</code> the rows <p>the five patterns worth memorising</p> <pre><code>// 1. connect
$conn = mysqli_connect("localhost", "root", "", "college");
if (!$conn) die("Connection failed: " . mysqli_connect_error());

// 2. select and loop
$result = mysqli_query($conn, "SELECT name, marks FROM students ORDER BY marks DESC");
if (!$result) die("Query failed: " . mysqli_error($conn));
while ($row = mysqli_fetch_assoc($result)) {
    echo htmlspecialchars($row["name"]), " — ", (int) $row["marks"], "&lt;br&gt;";
}

// 3. insert, safely
$stmt = mysqli_prepare($conn, "INSERT INTO students (roll, name, marks) VALUES (?, ?, ?)");
mysqli_stmt_bind_param($stmt, "isi", $roll, $name, $marks);
mysqli_stmt_execute($stmt);
echo mysqli_affected_rows($conn) . " row added";

// 4. one row only
$stmt = mysqli_prepare($conn, "SELECT * FROM students WHERE roll = ?");
mysqli_stmt_bind_param($stmt, "i", $roll);
mysqli_stmt_execute($stmt);
$row = mysqli_fetch_assoc(mysqli_stmt_get_result($stmt));
if (!$row) { echo "No such student"; }

// 5. search with LIKE — the wildcards go in the value
$pattern = "%" . $q . "%";
$stmt = mysqli_prepare($conn, "SELECT * FROM students WHERE name LIKE ?");
mysqli_stmt_bind_param($stmt, "s", $pattern);
</code></pre>

Question Function Returns Does it contain this? <code>str_contains($h, $n)</code> <code>true</code> / <code>false</code> — PHP 8, and it has no trap Where is it? <code>strpos($h, $n)</code> the position, or <code>false</code> — test with <code>===</code> Where is it, ignoring case? <code>stripos($h, $n)</code> same Where is the last one? <code>strrpos($h, $n)</code> same Does it start / end with this? <code>str_starts_with</code>, <code>str_ends_with</code> boolean — PHP 8 How many times? <code>substr_count($h, $n)</code> an integer Give me the rest from the match <code>strstr($h, $n)</code> the substring, or <code>false</code> Does it match a pattern? <code>preg_match($re, $s)</code> 1, 0 or <code>false</code> <p>Listing 2.7. Every search function on one string</p> <pre><code>&lt;?php
$h = "the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog";
$n = "the";

echo "haystack: $h\n";
echo "needle:   $n\n\n";

var_dump(str_contains($h, $n));
var_dump(strpos($h, $n));          // 0 — the very first character
var_dump(strrpos($h, $n));         // 31 — the last occurrence
var_dump(stripos($h, "THE"));      // case-insensitive
var_dump(substr_count($h, $n));    // 2
var_dump(str_starts_with($h, "the"));
var_dump(str_ends_with($h, "dog"));
var_dump(strstr($h, "fox"));       // from the match onward
var_dump(strstr($h, "fox", true)); // before the match

echo "\nAnd the trap, one more time:\n";
if (strpos($h, $n) == false)  echo "  == says: NOT FOUND (wrong)\n";
if (strpos($h, $n) === false) echo "  === says: not found\n";
else                          echo "  === says: found at ", strpos($h, $n), "\n";
